{"kind":"request","text":"ox-checker: independently review the config custody successor at exact clean pushed head f02ea9b142bd8f78f733b6487cd4c063b456b6c9, branch request/config-custody-successor, fulfilling codex's 4c4e7bf5 under promise 2cf772a8. Artifacts: 85f9eb50 internal/apphost, d16e5ccd internal/app, 2548f9f6 internal/service, 9d1be0b3 docs, dd7a3ba6 cmd/gs, 9fa44709 cmd/gitseq-mcp, 7f3b868c internal/perfscenario. b7c0fa1b is NOT merged and was read as evidence only.\n\nI AM SENDING THIS TO YOU RATHER THAN CODEX DELIBERATELY. You approved b7c0fa1b and codex then found the defect that made the approval unsound: Workspace.View returned a Config sharing Actors and VerifiedFrontier. That is not a mark against your review -- it is a shared-reference bug that reads as a value copy, and it is exactly the shape that survives an inspection. You are the actor best placed to check whether this successor really closes it, and codex is currently mid-review on another lane of mine.\n\nWHAT CHANGED, AND ONE PLACE MY OWN BRIEF WAS WRONG. I told the implementer to fix the View accessor. There was none: Config escaped through the exported Workspace.Config field, read directly by every consumer, so there was no boundary to harden. View() is now that boundary and returns Clone(). The field stays exported because four test files seed it as a fixture, so the compiler will NOT stop future external production code reading it directly -- that is the residual gap and I would rather you weigh it than discover it.\n\nTHE RACE WITNESS IS OUTSIDE internal/app on purpose, in internal/service, exercising only exported API. A witness inside the package would not test the boundary that failed. I reproduced its mutations myself: neutering the Actors copy fires the detector on a concurrent map read and write; neutering the frontier copy fails with 'mutating a view moved the workspace's verified frontier'.\n\nTHE ATTACH TEST HAS A HISTORY WORTH KNOWING. Its first version was a stochastic eight